well i i first have to say steve what an  absolute pleasure to meet you  you're one of those journalists i think  every journalist looks up to is inspired  by your storytelling it's magnificent  and i know viewers love that well thank  you wow  i was going to stay no matter what you  said  yeah i've gotta butter you up first no  i'm kidding um  you know talking about storytelling and  that's that's so important and it's so  critical especially when you have these  incredible shows they were able to take  part of  for you  what is it that makes a good story how  is it what is it that makes it the one  that's interesting because we do get a  lot of suggestions we go through a lot  of ideas we generally try to pick  something that when all is said and done  and the story's over that people will  feel something and ideally  we want people to feel overwhelming joy  because you know news in general doesn't  do a great job with joy we can do fear  pretty well and anger but joy can be  elusive sometimes but i think it's  really important and i think it's more  reflective of the real america than a  lot of what comes at the beginning of  the news guest so i just think it's  really important to have it in there and  if people end the newscast and our  stories always come at the end you know  feeling optimistic have their faith in  humanity restored then that's a good day  this franchise has been around for a  long time  does it make it easier now to weed  through some of these stories i assume  they're falling in your lap and you  don't have to go out and solicit for  them at this point  no they're not falling at our lap  media's so fractured now you know  there's so many people watching this and  that and so we know we don't get a lot  of people sending us ideas  but that's okay we'll we'll go out and  find them and and my producer scours the  local newspapers and goes to the  internet and tries to find just the  right thing to end the week so that that  is how it works it's still old-fashioned  journalism yes yeah it's not people  sending us ideas generally no that's  very interesting i would have thought  that that would have been so much easier  when charles corral did this job you  know 50 years ago when he started people  would send in things to cbs because it  was just cbs nbc and abc and everybody  watched charles grew up and he would  have a folder if he's going to  poughkeepsie he would have like a folder  of 100 story ideas he could do in  poughkeepsie it's not that way anymore  it's a little we have to sort of work a  little harder to find our ideas  and speaking of working a little bit  harder during the pandemic we all worked  a lot harder things changed so much for  us for you as you story tell around  america but you're not meeting these  folks face to face and you're doing it  virtually what was that like how did  that change your perspective it was  really hard you know i do on the road  and all of a sudden i'm in my house you  know it's not the nature of the segment  we did the best we could over zoom i  incorporated my children and did some  things with them because i'm stuck at  home and they're in my office hounding  me anyway might as well put them on tv  so we did some of that but i'm glad that  it's over because something's lost when  you can't go out and actually meet  people face to face and we're going  places that cbs news doesn't get to as  often it's sometimes it's the back roads  it's the smaller towns and i think it's  important to get out there and represent  those folks too it is important to to  represent all of america uh and you  mention your children they they have  certainly become part of your  storytelling so are we paying them yet  how's that going to work do they want to  be journalists good question are we  paying them no we're not but they do ask  sometimes  that started because he had stuck at  home and um schools around the country  were had been using our stories to teach  character education we we knew this so  that when the pandemic happened and kids  were stuck at home we started something  we called kindness 101  where we took stories and we sort of  framed them and used them to teach  lessons on on empathy or gratitude or  honesty integrity and  it was pretty successful but we found  that it was meant for kids but it was  mostly adults who were watching this  thing so now cbs is putting that on the  broadcast on cbs morning so my kids are  now on broadcast television and i know  that they're going to be asking for an  increase in allowance because of it  you're anticipating it yes  i love that and i love that you're able  to showcase a little bit of your family  that becomes real you know the reality  of what it's like to work at home for so  many people who were stuck yeah it was  really because they would come in and  they're they want to tell me everything  and you know  you don't have to work from home very  often but for those who do they know  it's very hard because you know you  don't get more than like a few minutes  at a time before somebody's asking you  for something or and i feel so guilty  that can you come outside and play with  us i i feel like i have to say yes every  time  and do you i do  and you're a good dad yeah i guess so so  does that come first dad and then  journalist yeah i tried i tried to do it  that way but it you know it can be  stressful  i think every parent can probably  understand that  what is it that you think your stories  do for people  the ones who are at home who are  watching  what do those stories give to them  what i'm trying to do through my work is  is remind people of what america is  really all about  and it's about people helping each other  people getting along whether they're  democrat or republican when you come  face to face with somebody you don't  care what their politics are you don't  care what their skin color is  people  like each other and i think we need to  be reminded of that and people are good  people are good  you know there are certain exceptions of  course but by and large people are good  and i just want to remind people of that  you know week in and week out  is that your goal for each story it is  it is  to reaffirm again restore faith in  humanity  and and present a true picture it's not  like i'm presenting just a rosy image of  america i feel like i'm presenting the  real picture of what america is all  about  i have to ask because we've the news  business has changed so much over the  years since i've been in it especially  since you've been in it  how is it that you've been able to  retain this feature  type  happy telling of stories you know the  feel good moments when the push is for  heart news hard and fast 20 seconds a  minute 20. how have you been able to  retain that  what's funny because you know as the  news has gotten harsher let's put it  that way i have worried off and on that  maybe my days are coming to an end as a  reporter because i thought there'd be  more like  there isn't room for this kind of  feel-good thing that i do it turned out  to be just the opposite it turned out  that there was more of an appetite for  it than ever and so now i'm not worried  about losing my job at this point  because i do i do feel like there will  always be an appetite for it and the  worse the world gets the more appetite  there is for it  so it is important  i think it is important you know the way  we structure our newscast you know it is  a construct we put the worst story that  whatever the worst thing that happened  that goes first for no logical reason  nobody ever said that and then the least  important which is usually the  life-affirming thing comes at the end  you know i've talked to my bosses before  i don't switch it around but and they  laugh at me but maybe someday  you just keep pushing keep pushing if  anyone can do it steve hartman can do it  i know it that's how it's going to work  i have to ask you and this and this is a  this is a tough question because you've  worked for so many years and done so  many incredible stories do you have one  that stands out is there one that is the  most memorable for you  yeah i am asked that sometimes and when  pressed i will say the story of nora and  dan  and that was a story about a little girl  who was in a grocery store and she saw  this grumpy old man and she asked him  for a hug  and what she didn't know what she would  have no way of knowing is that the man  had just lost his wife about six months  earlier and he'd given up his reason for  living  they hugged they became friends and they  were they saw each other every week and  gave him a renewed reason to live and i  like that story because it kind of  symbolizes all the stories  that we look for in that it was about as  insignificant little thing or seemingly  insignificant little thing that made a  huge impact and i think that's a theme  that you'll see through the on the road  stories you know week and week week out  is something that seems so little but it  somehow just really makes a big  difference in sometimes one person's  life and sometimes many people's lives  and so that's it when forced to choose i  usually choose that one beautiful story  and and tough because you've done so  many you have something that you're that  you're talking about for memorial day  speaking of trying to get everybody  involved you've been doing it a couple  years talk about  your endeavor for taps okay well this  started  many years ago i did a story we'd gotten  an email saying i keep hearing taps i  live in tacoma washington i don't know  where it's coming from so my producer at  the time said well he started digging  down let's try to figure this out we  found a guy named don britton who was  standing on his porch  every day at sunset and he was playing  taps and we went to do a story on this  like what his motivation was  and  what surprised us most on that shoot was  that when he would play  other people would come out and stand on  their balconies stand on their porches  and they would stand their hand over  heart and it was a beautiful thing and i  thought what if all of america could  feel what i was feeling in that moment  and years went by and then the pandemic  happened and  there were no memorial day parades or  ceremonies so we said maybe this is the  year so we started taps across america  and basically what it is is people who  play trumpet or bugle we ask them to  stand on their porches or in their front  yards and play taps and if you don't you  know go out there and just take take a  moment and we do it at three o'clock on  memorial day which is the moment of  remembrance and take a moment just to  reflect on what the holiday is all about  and we got a huge response and this is  gonna be the third year and we're we're  hoping this becomes like a national  tradition where you know long after i'm  gone this this this carries on music  does soothe the soul as i'm sure you  have discovered in so many of your  stories  is there anything that you want viewers  or other journalists to know about  yourself and about your path  um  that's funny because you know it's been  unusual having my kids  you know in the limelight with me and  people being able to see my family  because normally i don't like to focus  too much on on it and i still don't want  to because you know as a journalist  we're the storyteller right and  sometimes we do need to be involved in  the story but at the same time  the  it's about the people that we're  profiling and i wouldn't really  want any attention focused you know on  myself versus these incredible americans  that i get to meet and share their story  that's really what it's all about  always the humble storyteller thank you  thank you on the road right here in las  vegas we appreciate it thank you

